      "Every year, a large number of people are diagnosed with gallstones; however, many have no symptoms at all. When the gallbladder contracts, stones may block its outlet, causing pain in the upper abdomen, usually — but not necessarily — after fatty meals. The pain episode subsides with time. When a gallstone completely blocks the outlet of the gallbladder, it can lead to inflammation of the gallbladder, called cholecystitis.",
      "A gallstone can also block the duct that drains the gallbladder, causing cholangitis (inflammation and infection of the bile ducts), as well as obstruction of the duct that carries pancreatic enzymes, resulting in inflammation of the pancreas (pancreatitis). Pancreatitis can cause fever, severe abdominal pain and nausea. When complications from gallstones occur, cholecystectomy is required.",
